// `doc/backlog.md`'s "Explicit conversion between numeric types" item.
// `i32`/`f64`/`Complex` don't relate to each other as a subset/superset
// hierarchy the way ℕ ⊂ ℤ ⊂ ℝ ⊂ ℂ do in math -- nothing in the language lets
// one type widen into another. `Convert<From, To>` is an ordinary,
// two-target `algebra` (structurally identical to `MatMul<A, B, C>`, `To`
// an output-only generic never appearing in any parameter, resolved purely
// by a successful dispatch match) -- no new compiler mechanism needed for
// the algebra itself. `x.to()` (`lower.rs`'s own postfix-desugaring, same
// mechanism `a + b` already uses for `add(a, b)`) is sugar for `convert(x)`.
//
// Deliberately explicit-only (Rust `From`/`Into`-style), never implicit
// (C#-style implicit conversion was considered and rejected -- this
// project's own posture, applied everywhere else, is that nothing is
// guessed or silently inserted, always checked).
//
// Used to be deliberately dependency-free, like `stdlib/num`, specifically
// to work around `driver.rs`'s own former "one level deep only" `use`
// limitation (a `Convert<f64, Complex<f64>>` impl would have forced *every*
// `use convert;` program to also explicitly `use complex;`, even to reach
// the unrelated `f32 -> f64` impl below) -- `doc/backlog.md`'s own "Real
// transitive `use` resolution" item fixed that for real, so this
// restriction no longer applies. A `Convert<f64, Complex<f64>>` impl would
// be a perfectly reasonable addition now; just not added here opportunistically
// -- a user program wanting that conversion still declares its own impl for
// now (see `examples/convert.cleave`), matching this project's own posture
// that new numeric relationships are added via real `algebra`/`impl`
// declarations, never hardcoded into the compiler.
//
// `use convert;` explicitly -- not prelude (matches `io`/`complex`'s own
// precedent, not `num`'s): a program that never calls `.to()`/`convert(...)`
// sees zero change.

algebra Convert<From, To> {
    fn convert(x: From) -> To;
}

impl Convert<i32, f64> {
    fn convert(x) { mlir::arith::sitofp(x) }
}

impl Convert<i32, f32> {
    fn convert(x) { mlir::arith::sitofp(x) }
}

impl Convert<f32, f64> {
    fn convert(x) { mlir::arith::extf(x) }
}
